[s0l0!-header][h1]Overloading [/h1][/s0l0!-header]

Function [b]overloading [/b]allows to create multiple functions with the[b] same name[/b], so long as they have different parameters. 

For example, you might need a [b]printNumber()[/b] function that prints the value of its parameter.[code format="cpp" ]void printNumber([b]int [/b]a) { 
  cout << a;
}[/code]
This is effective with [b]integer [/b]arguments only. Overloading it will make it available for other types, such as [b]floats[/b].[code format="cpp" ]void printNumber([b]float [/b]a) { 
  cout << a;
}[/code]
[note]Now, the same [b]printNumber()[/b] function name will work for both integers and floats.[/note]

