[s0l0!-header][h1]Function Overloading[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

When overloading functions, the definition of the function must differ from each other by the types and/or the number of arguments in the argument list. 
[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="cpp" codeId="246"]void printNumber(int x) {
    cout << "Prints an integer: " << x << endl;
}
void printNumber(float x) {
    cout << "Prints a float: " << x << endl;
}
int main() {
  int a = 16;
  float b = 54.541;
  printNumber(a);
  printNumber(b);
}
[/code]
[note]As you can see, the function call is based on the argument provided. An [b]integer [/b]argument will call the function implementation that takes an [b]integer [/b]parameter. A [b]float [/b]argument will call the implementation taking a [b]float [/b]parameter.[/note]

